            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Done — replaced the `retry` poll with `poke_eventloop()`: wait for the saturating\r\nburst to drain, send the probe once, wait again, then assert. It's not just more\r\nidiomatic, it's more correct here — the fix only reclaims space once the buffer is\r\nfully empty, so the probe must land *after* the drain; `poke_eventloop()` guarantees\r\nthat ordering, whereas a retry could resend the probe against a still-pinned buffer.\r\n\r\nRe-verified locally with `make functionaltest` (`TEST_FILTER='keeps accepting input\r\nafter the input buffer fills and drains'`): it still bites — **fails on the unpatched\r\nbuild** (probe dropped, `Expected \"x\", Actual \"\"`, exit non-zero) and **passes on the\r\npatched build** (~47 ms). Same regression coverage as the `retry` version, just\r\nwithout the busy-poll.",
            "commit_id": "4c896bcc80118475288c056d814072f0fea0b238",
            "created_at": "2026-07-06T11:51:17Z",
            "diff_hunk": "@@ -820,6 +820,21 @@ describe('API', function()\n       command(\"call nvim_input('<M-'.nr2char(0x40000000).'>')\")\n       eq(1, eval('1'))\n     end)\n+\n+    it('keeps accepting input after the input buffer fills and drains', function()\n+      -- Regression test: the fixed input buffer (INPUT_BUFFER_SIZE == 16386) is\n+      -- compacted left, not a ring. Filling it to within <19 bytes of the top\n+      -- and then draining it used to leave the read/write cursors pinned, so\n+      -- input_enqueue() could never reclaim space and silently dropped all\n+      -- further input, freezing the editor. One oversized burst of a Normal-mode\n+      -- no-op (CTRL-L) saturates the buffer in a single nvim_input() call; after\n+      -- it drains, input must still register.\n+      api.nvim_input(('\\12'):rep(20000))\n+      t.retry(nil, nil, function()\n+        api.nvim_input('ix<Esc>')\n+        eq('x', api.nvim_get_current_line())\n+      end)",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40585#discussion_r3528592630",
